\title[Successor transfer on set partitions]{Successor Transfer on Canonically Ordered Set Partitions}
\author{Anonymous}
\date{}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
Order the blocks of a set partition by their minima.  In one successor-
transfer step, every nonsingleton block simultaneously sends its maximum to
the next block around a directed cycle.  We determine the finite dynamics of
this literal rule.  On partitions of $[n]$ into $k$ blocks, the maximum tail
is $\min\{n-2,2k-2\}$ for $1<k<n$; the one-block and all-singleton states are
fixed, and the global maximum is $n-2$.  Recurrent states have explicit dense
and sparse prefix--suffix forms, giving their exact counts, and every
nontrivial recurrent $k$-block state has period $k$.  Independently, the
one-step fibre of every target is the trace of a product of explicit
$5\times5$ matrices.  Its states record whether an incoming label is absent,
the sole element, the minimum, the maximum, or an interior element of a
target block.  This trace is sensitive to labelled interlacing: the targets
$025\mid134$ and $035\mid124$ have identical block size/minimum/maximum data
but fibres two and one.  Classical carrier and load mechanisms are treated as
background.  External circulation remains on hold.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{The literal rule and the theorem package}

Let $[n]=\{0,1,\ldots,n-1\}$, and let $\Pi_{n,k}$ be the set of its set
partitions into $k$ nonempty blocks.  We always write
\[
 \pi=(B_0,B_1,\ldots,B_{k-1}),\qquad
 \min B_0<\min B_1<\cdots<\min B_{k-1}.
\]
Define $\T:\Pi_{n,k}\to\Pi_{n,k}$ as follows.  Every block with at least two
elements removes its maximum and sends that element to $B_{i+1}$, with
subscripts modulo $k$; singleton blocks send nothing.  All removals and
additions occur simultaneously.  For $k=1$, removal and return occur in the
same block and $\T$ is the identity.

The canonical ordering in this definition is not an update-dependent
relabeling.  Indeed, each block retains its minimum.  Every element of the
new block $i+1$ is either from $B_{i+1}$ or is $\max B_i$, hence is strictly
larger than $\min B_i$, whereas the new block $i$ has minimum at most
$\min B_i$.  Thus adjacent output minima remain strictly increasing.  This
argument includes the donation $B_{k-1}\to B_0$: it affects the first block
but creates no last-to-first order comparison.

The restricted-growth word of $\pi$ is $w=w_0\cdots w_{n-1}$, where
$w_j=i$ exactly when $j\in B_i$.  Its first occurrences appear in the order
$0,1,\ldots,k-1$ \,\cite{Wachs1994}.  The literal rule becomes
\begin{equation}\label{eq:rgf-rule}
 \boxed{\quad\text{for every repeated letter $i$, replace its final
 occurrence by $i+1\pmod{k}$, simultaneously.}\quad}
\end{equation}
Every donating letter retains its original first occurrence.  If replacing
the last $i$ by $i+1$ creates an earlier first occurrence of $i+1$, that
position is still after the first $i$; the wrap to zero is harmless.  Hence
\eqref{eq:rgf-rule} remains a restricted-growth word on the same $k$ letters.

Restricted-growth encodings and their Stirling enumeration are background
\cite{Wachs1994}.  Whirling already supplies sequential local actions on
restricted-growth functions \cite{JosephProppRoby2025}; it is not
\eqref{eq:rgf-rule}.  The excess-load projection below is exactly a
threshold-one parallel chip-firing rule on a directed cycle and is likewise
background \cite{JiLiWang2025}.  We also distinguish the literal rule from
Bulgarian solitaire \cite{Brandt1982}, jeu-de-taquin promotion and rowmotion
\cite{Schutzenberger1972,StrikerWilliams2012}, box-ball dynamics
\cite{TakahashiSatsuma1990}, and set-partition stack sorting
\cite{ChoiEtAl2024}.  None of these carrier, load, sorting, counting, or
generic matrix mechanisms is counted as part of the residual theorem package.

For a point in a finite self-map, let $\tail(\pi)$ be its least entrance time
into a cycle.  Put $m=n-k$, let $\Stirling{m}{k}$ denote a Stirling number of
the second kind, and write $(k)_m=k!/(k-m)!$.

\begin{theorem}[sharp clocks and recurrent strata]\label{thm:temporal}
The successor-transfer map has the following complete temporal behavior.
\begin{enumerate}[(i)]
\item If $1<k<n$, then
\begin{equation}\label{eq:clock}
 \max_{\pi\in\Pi_{n,k}}\tail(\pi)=\min\{n-2,\,2k-2\}.
\end{equation}
For $k=1$ and $k=n$, the unique state is fixed.  Consequently, for every
$n\ge2$, the maximum over all set partitions of $[n]$ is $n-2$.

\item In the dense range $n\ge2k$, a $k$-block state is recurrent precisely
when its first $n-k$ letters form a surjective restricted-growth word on all
$k$ letters and its final $k$ letters are a permutation of them.  In the
sparse range $n\le2k$, it is recurrent precisely when its first $k$ letters
are $01\cdots(k-1)$ and its final $n-k$ letters are distinct.

\item The number $R_{n,k}$ of recurrent $k$-block states is
\begin{equation}\label{eq:recurrent-count}
 R_{n,k}=
 \begin{cases}
 k!\,\Stirling{n-k}{k},&n\ge2k,\\[2mm]
 (k)_{n-k},&n\le2k.
 \end{cases}
\end{equation}
At $n=2k$, both descriptions read $01\cdots(k-1)$ followed by a
permutation, and both counts equal $k!$.  Every recurrent state with
$1<k<n$ has exact period $k$.  The cases $k=1$ and $k=n$ have period one.
Thus for $n\ge2$ the possible periods over all strata are exactly
$1,2,\ldots,n-1$; at $n=1$ the unique state is fixed.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

\section{The directed-cycle load factor}

Let $c_i$ be the multiplicity of letter $i$ and set
\[
 z_i=c_i-1\ge0,\qquad \sum_{i=0}^{k-1}z_i=m.
\]
Writing $b_i=\ind_{\{z_i>0\}}$, \eqref{eq:rgf-rule} gives
\begin{equation}\label{eq:queue}
 z_i(t+1)=z_i(t)-b_i(t)+b_{i-1}(t),
 \qquad i\pmod{k}.
\end{equation}
This entire factor is the directed-cycle chip-firing mechanism already
subtracted above.  We record its finite-time lemma because the labelled lift
needs an exact entrance bound.

\begin{lemma}[queue smoothing]\label{lem:smoothing}
Assume $m>0$.  If $m\le k$, then every $z_i$ is in $\{0,1\}$ after at most
$m-1$ steps.  If $m\ge k$, then every $z_i$ is positive after at most $k-1$
steps.  The resulting sparse and dense regimes are forward invariant.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Lift indices periodically to $\mathbb Z$.  Choose integers $H_i(0)$ with
\[
 H_i(0)-H_{i-1}(0)=z_i(0),\qquad H_{i+k}(0)=H_i(0)+m.
\]
Since $z_i$ is a nonnegative integer, \eqref{eq:queue} is equivalent to
\begin{equation}\label{eq:maxplus-step}
 H_i(t+1)=\max\{H_i(t)-1,H_{i-1}(t)\}.
\end{equation}
Induction on $t$ yields the explicit solution
\begin{equation}\label{eq:maxplus-solution}
 H_i(t)=\max_{0\le r\le t}
       \bigl(H_{i-r}(0)-(t-r)\bigr).
\end{equation}
Fix $i,t$ and put $X_r=H_{i-r}(0)+r$.  Taking the difference at adjacent
indices in \eqref{eq:maxplus-solution} gives
\begin{equation}\label{eq:two-maxima}
 z_i(t)=1+\max_{0\le r\le t}X_r-
             \max_{1\le r\le t+1}X_r.
\end{equation}
The two maxima share $X_1,\ldots,X_t$.  If $z_i(t)=0$, the second maximum
must be the endpoint $X_{t+1}$ and exceed the first by one; hence
\[
 \sum_{r=0}^{t}z_{i-r}(0)=H_i(0)-H_{i-t-1}(0)\le t.
\]
If $z_i(t)\ge2$, the first maximum must instead be $X_0$ and exceed the
second by at least one; hence
\begin{equation}\label{eq:cones}
 \sum_{r=0}^{t}z_{i-r}(0)\ge t+2.
\end{equation}

For $m\le k$, take $t=m-1$.  A surviving value $z_i(t)\ge2$ would force at
least $m+1$ units in a cone although the total mass is $m$.  For $m\ge k$,
take $t=k-1$.  An empty $z_i(t)$ would bound the mass in one complete cycle
by $k-1$, contradicting $m\ge k$.  Finally, a binary load obeys
$z_i(t+1)=z_{i-1}(t)$, while an everywhere-positive load is fixed under
\eqref{eq:queue}; both regimes are invariant.
\end{proof}

\section{Labelled windows, recurrence, and sharpness}

Load smoothing does not yet make the labelled partition recurrent.  A second
phase orders a distinguished word window.

\begin{lemma}[canonical windows]\label{lem:windows}
After Lemma~\ref{lem:smoothing}, at most $k-1$ further steps put the state in
one of the following invariant forms.
\begin{enumerate}[(a)]
\item If $m\ge k$, the final $k$ word positions form a permutation of all
$k$ letters.
\item If $m\le k$, the first $k$ word positions are $01\cdots(k-1)$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
In the dense regime every letter remains repeated.  Reverse the word and
inspect its first $k$ positions, equivalently the original final $k$
positions.  For every colour present in this window, exactly its first
reversed occurrence moves one site clockwise under \eqref{eq:rgf-rule}; an
absent colour contributes no moving window token.  The occupancy vector
therefore follows \eqref{eq:queue}, now with mass exactly $k$.  The dense
case of Lemma~\ref{lem:smoothing} makes all $k$ occupancies positive by time
$k-1$, hence each is one.

In the sparse regime every full-word multiplicity is one or two.  Let $q_i$
count letter $i$ in the first $k$ positions.  Then
$q_i\in\{0,1,2\}$ and $\sum_iq_i=k$.  A value two has both copies in the
prefix and moves its later copy clockwise; a value at most one does not move
a prefix token.  Thus
\begin{equation}\label{eq:prefix-particles}
 q_i'=q_i-\ind_{\{q_i=2\}}+\ind_{\{q_{i-1}=2\}}.
\end{equation}
Regard each $2$ as one excess particle and each $0$ as a hole.  Particles
move one site per step through occupied sites, preserve cyclic order, and
annihilate on the first unmatched hole.  Pairing particles with holes in
cyclic order on the universal cover shows that every pair meets within
$k-1$ steps.  Then all $q_i=1$.  A restricted-growth word whose first $k$
letters are a permutation of $0,\ldots,k-1$ must have those letters in the
order $01\cdots(k-1)$.
